                    [145] Further, I must also respectfully disagree with Justice Kasirer’s contention that <b>RJR‐MacDonald</b> is distinguishable from this case. To the contrary, I believe that <b>RJR‐MacDonald</b> provides a full answer to this case. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[148] Here, like in <b>RJR‐MacDonald</b>, Parliament employed indirect means to suppress the threat to health it identified. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[249] Of course, Parliament can use indirect means in the exercise of its criminal law power, as was the case in <b>RJR-MacDonald</b>. Yet, contrary to the amicus curiae’s suggestion, <b>RJR-MacDonald</b> is of little assistance in this case. 
