                <p class="snippet" >
                    Also McLachlin J., in writing for the majority in <b>RJR-MacDonald</b> Inc. v. Canada (Attorney General), [1995] 3 S.C.R. 199, at para. 137, expressed the unanimous view of the Court: &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;In <b>RJR-MacDonald</b>, supra, at para. 160, McLachlin J. stated: “if the government fails to explain why a significantly less intrusive and equally effective measure was not chosen, the law may fail” (emphasis added). &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;This sets this case apart from cases involving advertisers (such as Irwin Toy, Libman or <b>RJR-MacDonald</b>), where advertisers encouraged choices which served their particular interests. 
